   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the vehicle and traffic law, in relation to creating an exemption to the snowmobile registration requirements by creating a seasonal trail pass pilot program for Canadian citizens; and providing for the repeal of such provisions upon expiration thereof",
    "basePrintNo": "S5129",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5129-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 45,
       "fullName": "Betty Little",
       "imgName": "379_betty_little.jpg",
       "incumbent": false,
       "memberId": 379,
       "sessionMemberId": 947,
       "sessionYear": 2017,
       "shortName": "LITTLE"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §2222, V & T L",
    "lawSection": "Vehicle and Traffic Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S5129\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the vehicle and traffic law, in\nrelation to creating an exemption to the snowmobile registration\nrequirements by creating a seasonal trail pass pilot program for\nCanadian citizens; and providing for the repeal of such provisions\nupon expiration thereof\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nTo allow Canadian snowmobilers a quick and convenient way to ride in\nNew York State without having to go through the current, cumbersome\nregistration process.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1: Amends subdivision five of section 2222 of the vehicle and\ntraffic law to exempt Canadian riders from the current registration\nprocess, but requires them to comply with a new section of law created\nby this act\n\nSection 2: Amends subdivision section 2222 of the vehicle and traffic\nlaw by adding a new subdivision, 5-a, which describes the trail pass\npilot program.\n\nSection 3: States that the act shall take effect on the ninetieth day\nafter it shall become law and shall expire on January I, 2024.\n\nSection three also authorizes the Commissioner of the Department of\nMotor Vehicles to amend or repeal any regulation necessary for the\nimplementation of the act.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nNew York State draws nearly twenty-thousand out of state riders to its\nworld famous trails every year, generating over three-quarters of a\nmillion dollars in revenue from the registrations these riders must\nobtain. This figure is only a part of the massive economic impact\ngenerated by these tourists, because it does not include the hotel\nrooms, meals, fuel, and other purchases made by these tourists in\ncommunities across the state. For U.S. citizens from other states a\nnew DMV tool has made it possible to instantly download and print a\ntemporary registration, wherever they are, and then head to the\ntrails.  However, New York State's Canadian neighbors cannot take\nadvantage of this process. Canadian riders must physically present\nthemselves at a DMV office to obtain a registration, a process that\ncan take several hours and might not result in the immediate issuance\nof a registration. Also, DMV offices are closed on weekends, when most\nCanadian tourists are likely to want to travel to New York State. As a\nresult, despite being so close to many of New York State's best trail\nsystems, very few Canadian riders are compelled to make the journey\nsouth. In fact, of the 17,373 out of state riders that enjoyed New\nYork State's trails in 2015 only ninety-six of them were Canadian.\n\nThe trail pass system proposed by this act would help to reduce the\nbarriers to Canadian riders, making New York State a more accessible\ndestination. Trail passes will cost the same as state-issued\nregistrations, so neither the State nor snowmobile clubs will be\ndenied any revenue. Also, the pass will be available instantly on the\npresentation of a valid Canadian registration at any departmentally\napproved retailer. Canadians must present, on request, significant\nproof of identity and ownership when transporting their snowmobile\nacross the border. There is no need to subject them to a redundant\nevaluation at a DMV office. Furthermore, by making it easier for\nCanadians to ride in New York State this program will increase the\n\neconomic impact of tourist-riders by increasing the number of Canadian\nriders who make the journey south.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nNew bill.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nTo be determined\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect within 90 days.\n",
