    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S5129A\n\nSPONSOR: RITCHIE\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the vehicle and traffic law, in\nrelation to creating an exemption to the snowmobile registration\nrequirements by creating a seasonal trail pass pilot program for Canadi-\nan citizens; and providing for the repeal of such provisions upon expi-\nration thereof\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo allow Canadian snowmobilers a quick and convenient way to ride in New\nYork State without having to go through the current, cumbersome regis-\ntration process.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1: Amends subdivision five of section 2222 of the vehicle and\ntraffic law to exempt Canadian riders from the current registration\nprocess, but requires them to comply with a new section of law created\nby this act\n\nSection 2: Amends subdivision section 2222 of the vehicle and traffic\nlaw by adding a new subdivision, 5-a, which describes the trail pass\npilot program.\n\nSection 3: States that the act shall take effect on the ninetieth day\nafter it shall become law and shall expire on January 1, 2025.  Section\nthree also authorizes the Commissioner of the Department of Motor Vehi-\ncles to amend or repeal any regulation necessary for the implementation\nof the act.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nNew York State draws nearly twenty-thousand out of state riders to its\nworld famous trails every year, generating over three-quarters of a\nmillion dollars in revenue from the registrations these riders must\nobtain. This figure is only a part of the massive economic impact gener-\nated by these tourists, because it does not include the hotel rooms,\nmeals, fuel, and other purchases made by these tourists in communities\nacross the state. For U.S. citizens from other states a new DMV tool has\nmade it possible to instantly download and print a temporary registra-\ntion, wherever they are, and then head to the trails.  However, New York\nState's Canadian neighbors cannot take advantage of this process. Cana-\ndian riders must physically present themselves at a DMV office to obtain\na registration, a process that can take several hours and might not\nresult in the immediate issuance of a registration. Also, DMV offices\nare closed on weekends, when most Canadian tourists are likely to want\nto travel to New York State. As a result, despite being so close to many\nof New York State's best trail systems, very few Canadian riders are\ncompelled to make the journey south. In fact, of the 17,373 out of state\nriders that enjoyed New York State's trails in 2015 only ninety-six of\nthem were Canadian.\n\nThe trail pass system proposed by this act would help to reduce the\nbarriers to Canadian riders, making New York State a more accessible\ndestination. Trail passes will cost the same as state-issued registra-\ntions, so neither the State nor snowmobile clubs will be denied any\nrevenue. Also, the pass will be available instantly on the presentation\nof a valid Canadian registration at any departmentally approved retail-\ner. Canadians must present, on request, significant proof of identity\nand ownership when transporting their snowmobile across the border.\nThere is no need to subject them to a redundant evaluation at a DMV\noffice. Furthermore, by making it easier for Canadians to ride in New\nYork State this program will increase the economic impact of tourist-\nriders by increasing the number of Canadian riders who make the journey\nsouth.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nTo be determined\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect within 90 days.\n",
